   ],
   "source": [
    "def by_rater_pair(p):\n",
    "    rows = []\n",
    "    for (ra, rb), g in p.groupby([\"rater_a\", \"rater_b\"]):\n",
    "        ag = [ (g.loc[usable(g, it), f\"a_{it}\"] == g.loc[usable(g, it), f\"b_{it}\"]).mean() for it in ALL]\n",
    "        rows.append(dict(rater_a=ra, rater_b=rb, pairs=len(g), mean_agreement=np.nanmean(ag)))\n",
    "    return pd.DataFrame(rows).sort_values(\"mean_agreement\")\n",
    "for k, p in COMPARISONS.items():\n",
    "    if len(p):\n",
    "        display(Markdown(f\"**{k}**\"))\n",
    "        display(by_rater_pair(p).style.format({\"mean_agreement\": \"{:.1%}\"}).hide(axis=\"index\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f3f584e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In **Field vs Street View**, a pair can be one person's own field and Street View ratings (for example Michael with Michael). Those pairs measure the method, not two different raters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e42fccdb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### How each rater uses the ordered items\n",
    "The share of each rater's answers in each category. A rater whose answers pile up on one value (Q13 = 2 everywhere, say) usually points to a different reading of the protocol, not to what they saw."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
